+### Speed wave 3: S3-CREDITS, the credits parade (2026-11-15)
+
+Speed audit 3's credits item: the parade at 26.3 fps (32 frames of 50 ms), and on today's tip 24.1 fps (37.4 ms of work) with
+the sprite governor (`GBA_GOV_MS_N` 29 ms) dropping every marcher but the lead. A parade frame is ~21 ms of ground, card, ticker
+and confetti plus 3 ms of body lookups plus the body jobs (a rank comes on every 11 ticks, 2 bodies of ~26 ms each: ~5 ms a tick
+on average), and every marcher drawn costs ~0.8 ms: all 45 sprites with the governor off took 59 ms. So the steps below first
+cut what did not change, then trade the ground for sprites.
+
+**Exact steps** (700 frames of the parade without marchers hash-identical to the tip before: `vhash-seq` under `sync on`,
+three windows of 200 / 300 / 200 ticks; the tests below):
+
+* `look_run_hash` (`look_pool.c`, cross-stream) returns 0 when the pool has no run for the id (the set is all zeros and
+  `deep_hash` of zeros is 0): `bodyspr_image` resolved and hashed 21 fields for every marcher on every frame (4.9% of the
+  frame). Parade 37.4 -> 34.7 ms on its own.
+* The ticker bar is a retained panel (`PANEL_OW_OBJECTIVE`: its pixels change when the middle rank does) and the card's
+  heading plate is `ui_title_kept()` (new in `ui.c`: `ui_title` as a panel keyed by text, style and the sparkles' phase, so one
+  frame in three is drawn; an inline `ui_title` on the PS1). The card's lines lie on the moving parade and cannot be kept.
+* No library divisions: the ARM7 has no divide, and in Thumb every `%` or `/` is a ~100-cycle call, by a constant too. The
+  confetti (60 a frame) and the fans (24) now take one remainder of the tick and running sums (`cr_confetti_xy`,
+  `cr_fans_xj`, checked against the old closed forms for 1,500+ ticks at three screen sizes).
+* The ground pass compares one word of the cell (wall, floor texture, light, floor height: `ow_cell_key`) against the last
+  ground cell's instead of four byte loads and three compares (~2.5 -> ~1 ms of `plat_overworld`).
+* `bodyspr_image`'s per-id hint slot (`id & 63`) made Hillvile rank n and Hellvile 500 + n share a slot for four ranks of
+  every 16 on screen, which scanned all 48 memo slots; Hellvile's ids now start 32 slots on.
+
+**Pixel-changing trades (GBA only, the owner's VISUAL TRADE-OFFS APPROVED: "a lighter parade ground")**
+
+1. **The ground is flat runs** (`DoomoOverworldView.lite`, set by `credits.c`; read by the GBA's `plat_overworld` only, so the
+   PS1, Dreamcast and native pixels are unchanged): each open ground cell is filled with its texture's average colour at its
+   light level (`texture_avg`, the colormap), equal neighbours in one `vid_rect`; no lit tile cache, no 16-pixel block
+   copies. Raised cells (trees, walls), the sprites, the card, the ticker, the confetti and the fireworks are as before. The
+   grass, the road, the river and the bridge keep their colours and places; the grass's dither and flowers are gone.
+   ~5 ms a frame, which the sprite governor spends on marchers (it was 0-1 visible, now 6-8 around the middle).
+2. **A parade body may take 7 ticks** (`PC_BODY_SPAN`, `portrait.c`): a body job behind a stand-in is due in `PC_STANDIN` (4)
+   ticks and made 6 of every 11 ticks 8-15 ms heavier (frames of 34-45 ms: 3 vblanks, and a governor that dropped every
+   marcher). The deadline is only the latest: the idle time after a frame finishes most bodies early. GBA, `bodyspr_lazy(3)`
+   only (the PS1 keeps 4). Measured over a 4,000-tick run with a counter in `build()` (not kept): the marchers in view whose
+   body was not ready were 26 of 166,000 asks at 7 ticks and 10 of 163,000 at 5 (4 ticks: the tip's way), so a rank, which
+   is asked 11 ticks before it walks in, is ready when it comes on at both.
+
+Before / after (`credits.txt` = `speed2_story.txt`'s credits windows alone; libmgba, `sync on 61`; screenshots in the
+evidence folder of the package: `before_series/c1..c4.png`, `after_series/c1..c4.png`, ticks 300, 700, 1,100, 1,500 of the credits):
+
+| window | before (tip `cf5e9f2`) | after |
+|---|---|---|
+| credits (first 130 ticks) | 28.6 fps, 32.8 / 48.6 ms work, 12 frames of 50 ms | **29.8 fps**, 28.1 / 41.5 ms, 2 frames of 50 ms |
+| credits parade | 24.1 fps, 37.4 / 52.6 ms, 51 frames of 50 ms, the lead alone on the road | **30.0 fps**, 28.0 / 37.4 ms, worst frame 34 ms, 6-8 marchers and the fans |
+
+Step by step on the parade (fps, work avg ms): run hash 25.4 (34.7), panels + divisions + ground key 26.7 (33.4), flat ground
+29.0 (29.1), body span 5 29.3-29.5 (28.1-28.5), hint slot 29.5 (28.1), span 7 **30.0** (28.0). No frame is over 50 ms in
+either window. Floors: `expect-fps 29` (credits) and `28` (parade), and `expect-2x2` on a flat patch of grass (20 mixed blocks
+against 127 of the textured ground) in `speed2_story.txt`. `core/tests`: `test_panel` plays the parade (card heading, ticker)
+with the panel cache on and off at 240x160 and 320x240, every frame identical; `test_portrait_s27` checks the run hash, the
+confetti and the fans against the old formulas.
+
+**Still short.** The two windows are at 30.0 and 29.8, but the goal was a parade with no marcher dropped, and the GBA shows
+about a third of the ~45 sprites (the governor, `GBA_GOV_MS_N` 29 ms): a marcher costs ~0.8 ms in Mode 4 (all of them with the
+governor off took 59 ms, 16 fps), and a parade frame without any sprite is still ~20 ms (the flat ground 3 ms, the card and
+ticker 3 ms, the confetti and fireworks 1.5 ms) plus ~5 ms of body jobs and ~3 ms of lookups. The first 130 ticks keep two 50 ms
+frames (the opening line-up's bodies). What would close it: a cheaper body for a marcher (the jobs are the portrait renderer's:
+`finish_marked`, `sh_span`, `capsule_plain`), marchers as OBJs (a tile-mode frame measured 78 ms with this scene's ~150-tile
+canvas: the card, the ticker and the confetti) or a slower pace (`RANK_TICKS` 11 -> 16: fewer bodies a second and fewer
+marchers on screen). Not done: the card's lines as a panel (they lie on the moving parade; a plate behind them would keep
+them, a further pixel trade).
+
